The Gherkin representation is chosen for its readability and alignment with behavioral specifications, which is suitable for capturing the user interactions and acceptance criteria. It is also accessible for non-technical stakeholders to understand and contribute to the test scenarios.

The xUnit Test Cases are selected for their ability to write executable tests that directly validate the system's behavior, including the handling of exceptions and data modifications. This representation is essential for ensuring that the system behaves as expected when speciality or doctor IDs are missing or when attempting to delete a doctor with open consultation requests.

Both representations are chosen for their relevance to the functional requirements and their ability to effectively test the specified scenarios. The Gherkin representation is more aligned with the initial specification and user interaction level, while the xUnit Test Cases are chosen for their technical depth and ability to assert the expected outcomes at the code level. The simplicity and directness of xUnit Test Cases make them reliable for writing valid tests, while the Gherkin representation provides a high-level overview that is easy to understand and maintain.
